findings.description:
Correction 2 defines "file-level" three ways in one section:

- the heading says "carries a `filePath`";
- line 151 says "the file-level selector becomes an explicit type allow-list";
- lines 155-158 give a deny-list and say "stated as an exclusion rather than an allow-list deliberately".

The architecture's read discipline defines file-level as nine named types. The deny-list means a future fine-grained upstream type is silently included. That is the context blow-up the discipline exists to prevent, and it fails quietly where the architecture requires upstream drift to fail loudly.

The "no `filePath` → drift" guard only helps if such a type lacks `filePath`, which the design has not established. Use the architecture's nine-type list, and report any type outside those nine plus `function`/`class` as drift. Then fix the three statements to agree.
